Flat directory mode: every `.hll` file sitting directly in the
directory is its own entry point, and each one's output lands in
`<out>/<file stem>/docker-compose.yml`. `--out` is required here,
because there is no single sensible default location for the output of
however many files. `build-directory.out/` holds both generated
documents, so this case asserts what was written across the whole tree
rather than reading each file back one at a time.

`services/networks.hll` declares no service. It is a library file, so
it is skipped rather than compiled into a service-less document, and
the run stays silent about it.

```console
$ hllc build services --out dist
dist/api/docker-compose.yml
dist/web/docker-compose.yml

```

A directory in which *nothing* declares a service is not an error — a
build that produces no output still exits 0 — but it says so, because
silently doing nothing is indistinguishable from a broken invocation.

The two blocks together check the "nothing was built" message from both
directions, which is what protects the running `built` counter. A
mutation that swaps its `== 0` test makes the first block print the
message where the transcript expects silence; one that stops the
counter incrementing makes the message appear in both.

```console
$ hllc build library --out dist
library: none of the .hll files here declare a service — nothing was built

```
